# Phase 099 — Counter, defaultdict, deque and named records

## What changed

The finding reproduced on the phase base before any edit: `collections` was not
a module, so `collections.counter([1, 1])` failed with `Unknown variable
'collections'`, and a missing record field answered `nothing` rather than a
documented default. The new names are builtins reached through the shared
`runtime::builtin` dispatcher, so the tree-walker and the bytecode VM answer
through one implementation. No `Value` variant, error variant or grammar rule
changed.

This resume kept the preserved implementation and finished the evidence the
definition of done still lacked: a structural cost test for `list.shift` against
a deque work queue, a single-entry Counter test, a linear deque constructor, and
balanced deque rebalancing that keeps alternating pops from rebuilding the untouched side.

The final resume fixed the one part of the deque claim the preserved work did not
actually meet. Every operation used to walk every node of both ends, so a deque
was O(n) per push or pop despite the tree structure. A deque is now an opaque
`Object` that Redblue source cannot construct; its trees are trusted after
construction, while a hand-written record with the same fields is still
accepted and still validated all the way down. That preserves every forged-tree
refusal and removes the per-operation walk from the module's own deques. The
result is measured, not asserted. The preserved tree's `collections_test` binary
took 285 s. After this round's changes the same binary has 45 tests and, built
in debug mode with `cargo test --test collections_test` on the CI runner used for
this phase, ran them in 5.92 s. The earlier 4.2 s figure belonged to a 35-test
binary and is not comparable with the final binary, so it is not used as evidence.

The review then closed the budget and representation gaps that resume left. A
deque now enforces `MAX_DEQUE_ITEMS` in the constructor and after every push,
so a list longer than the cap is refused before any tree is built and a push at
the cap cannot produce a deque no operation can use. An over-budget refusal
names the limit and the length that crossed it rather than calling the value a
non-deque, and the trusted object path now requires the same `kind: "deque"`
marker the record path does. `json.stringify` refuses a deque instead of
silently writing `null` for it, and `SPEC.md` names the `kind: "deque"` marker a
hand-written record needs.

The final resume closed one remaining inconsistency in the forged-record path.
A hand-written Counter listing the same key twice used to answer the first
entry's count from `collections.count` while `collections.most_common` listed
both, and a hand-written defaultdict with a repeated key let `collections.put`
update the first copy while leaving a stale second one. `collection_pairs` now
refuses a repeated key with a catchable `RuntimeError` naming it. The check
keeps a hash index, so it is expected-linear and a large Counter or defaultdict
is not re-scanned once per pair. A bucket that many distinct keys share is still
resolved by equality and costs one comparison per earlier key in that bucket,
so the worst case remains quadratic; a unit test forces two distinct keys into
one bucket and proves they are kept, while an equal pair in the same bucket is
still refused.

The review then closed the representation leak in deque equality. `Value`'s
derived equality compared a deque's `front` and `back` trees structurally, so
`collections.deque([1, 2, 3, 4])` and the same sequence built by four
`push_right` calls were unequal even though both pop the same items.
`Value` now compares two module deques by their logical items, and
`group_value_hash` hashes them the same way, so `is`, `==`, Counter and
defaultdict keys, `groupby` and `lru_cache` keys all agree no matter how the
deque was built. The comment on `stack_from_values` now says its trees are
trusted because source cannot construct the `Object`, rather than claiming a
validation the constructor does not perform.

This final resume closed the last obvious hole in the deque surface: a deque had
no size. Every other collection in the language can be measured — a list and a
text with `length`, a record by its fields — but `length(q)` on a deque failed,
so a program holding one could only count items by popping it. `length` now
answers a deque's logical item count from the sizes stored on the spine, without
walking the items. A value that claims to be a deque is validated by the same
`deque_parts` every other operation uses, so a malformed or over-budget
hand-written record is refused with the usual deque error rather than measured.
The ordinary "length requires a list or text" error for every other value is
unchanged, which keeps the differential corpus's recorded error text intact.

| File | Lines | What |
|---|---|---|
| `src/runtime.rs` | +121 −28 (resume, review and final resume) | `collection_pairs`, the Counter/defaultdict/deque helpers, persistent stack trees for deque ends, named-record validation, the thirteen `collections.*` arms, a linear `stack_from_values` deque constructor, and `deque_rebalance`, which splits a non-empty side across both ends instead of reversing the whole remainder. The resume makes `deque_value` build an opaque `Object` and splits `deque_parts` into a trusted object path (cheap shape and budget checks) and an untrusted record path (full `validate_stack_tree` walk), so module deques no longer re-walk every node per operation while hand-written records are still refused node by node. The review enforces the item budget in the constructor and after each push, splits malformed-value errors from a dedicated budget error that names the limit and supplied length, requires the `kind: "deque"` marker on the trusted object path as well as the record path, corrects the `stack_cons` trust-boundary comment, and makes `json_stringify` refuse a deque rather than write `null`; the final resume adds the expected-linear duplicate-key check in `collection_pairs`, and this review adds logical deque equality and hashing, the collision test, and the corrected `stack_from_values` trust comment; this round adds a private marker value only a module-made `Object` can carry, so a source-declared `object deque` cannot enter the trusted path, and replaces the eager equality/hash vectors with lazy tree cursors that compare in lockstep and fold one item at a time; this final resume adds `looks_like_deque` and `deque_length`, so `length(deque)` answers from the spine sizes after the same validation every other operation performs |
